  "body": "my id is @eric:[matrix.example.com](http://matrix.example.com/), and [example.com](http://example.com/) are now used by a website, Can I use @eric:[example.com](http://example.com/) as my ID?\r\n\r\nI am now using delegate:\r\n````\r\nmatrix.example.com {\r\n  # enable logging\r\n  log\r\n\r\n  # .well-known is delegated from example.com and served here\r\n  header /.well-known/matrix/server {\r\n    Content-Type application/json\r\n  }\r\n  respond /.well-known/matrix/server 200 {\r\n    body \"{ \\\"m.server\\\": \\\"matrix.example.com:443\\\" }\"\r\n    close\r\n  }\r\n  # .well-known delegate for client\r\n  header /.well-known/matrix/client {\r\n    Content-Type application/json\r\n  }\r\n  respond /.well-known/matrix/client 200 {\r\n    body \"{\\\"m.homeserver\\\": {\\\"base_url\\\": \\\"https://matrix.example.com\\\"}}\"\r\n    close\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  reverse_proxy /_matrix/* http://localhost:8008\r\n  reverse_proxy /_synapse/client/* http://localhost:8008\r\n  reverse_proxy localhost:8008\r\n}\r\n````\r\nI really don't like the subdomain matrix.example.com in my ID, It's very ugly. and I want to beautify the ID url. \r\nAnyone know how to figure this requirement out? ",
